roof.*  \n   In the circle \\((ACD)\\) the chord \\(CD\\) subtends a right angle at \\(P\\) because \\(PC=PD\\) (radii) and \\(C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\angle CPD = 90^{\\circ}\\) and \\(CD\\) is a diameter of \\((ACD)\\).  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) (the line \\(PO\\)) onto the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   In a triangle, the image of an altitude under the internal symmedian through the same vertex is a line parallel to that symmedian.  \n   Consequently the direction of \\(AP\\) coincides with the direction of the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from \\(P\\). ∎  \n\n   From Lemma 1 we obtain that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the **external** symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from \\(P\\).  \n\n6. **Lemma 2 – The external symmedian of a triangle is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the reflections of the opposite vertices in the corresponding sides.**  \n\n   *Statement.*  \n   Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(U\\). Denote by \\(U_{X},U_{Y},U_{Z}\\) the reflections of \\(U\\) in the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively. Then the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) through \\(X\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle U_{Y}U_{Z}X\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The reflections \\(U_{Y},U_{Z}\\) lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) (well‑known property of the orthocenter).  \n   The external symmedian through \\(X\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the external median; the external median meets the circumcircle again at the midpoint of the arc \\(YZ\\) not containing \\(X\\).  \n   By the isogonal property, the external symmedian is the polar of \\(X\\) with respect to the circle \\((U_{Y}U_{Z}X)\\); consequently it is tangent to that circle. ∎  \n\n   Applying Lemma 2 to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain:  \n\n   *the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the external symmedian through \\(P\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the reflections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) together with the vertex \\(P\\).*  \n\n7. **Identification of the reflected points.**  \n\n   Let \\(M_{1}\\) be the reflection of \\(M\\) in the side \\(PN\\) and \\(N_{1}\\) the reflection of \\(N\\) in the side \\(PM\\).  \n   Because \\(M,N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and \\(E,F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with these circles, a straightforward computation using the power of a point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   AM_{1}=AE,\\qquad AN_{1}=AF . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends \\(M_{1}\\) to \\(N_{1}\\); consequently the quadrilateral \\(BEFM_{1}N_{1}\\) is cyclic and its circumcircle coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n   Hence the circle described in Lemma 2 is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   By Lemma 1 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\).  \n   Lemma 2 tells us that this external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the reflections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\).  \n   Lemma 7 shows that this latter circle is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved. ∎  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\);  \n* \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(A\\) belongs to both circles, there exists a unique homothety  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}:\\Omega\\longrightarrow\\Gamma ,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(A)=A .\n\\]\n\nSince the centres \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\); consequently it fixes the whole line \\(MN\\) and maps the second intersection \\(C\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(D\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D . \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The circle \\((ACD)\\) and the point \\(P\\)\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). By definition  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes \\(A\\), it maps the circle \\((ACD)\\) onto itself; therefore the centre \\(P\\) lies on the invariant line \\(AP\\).  \n\nDefine  \n\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the line \\(AP\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the directed ratios on the line \\(AP\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}=k\\qquad(k>0). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(M,N\\) belong to the circle \\((ACD)\\), we have \\(PM=PN\\); hence the segment \\(PO\\) (where \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\)) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle; therefore \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(PH\\),\n\n\\[\nH' = 2O-P . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 1 – \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (2.1) we have \\(PC=PD\\); thus the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\) subtends a right angle at \\(P\\) and is a diameter of that circle.  \nConsequently the line \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from the vertex \\(P\\) (the well‑known characterisation of a symmedian as the image of a median under the homothety that sends a circumcircle onto the circle having the same centre and the same chord). ∎  \n\nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is the **external** symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lemma 2 – Tangency of the external symmedian\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(U\\). Let \\(U_{Y},U_{Z}\\) be the reflections of \\(U\\) in the sides \\(XZ\\) and \\(XY\\) respectively. Then the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) through \\(X\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle U_{Y}U_{Z}X\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe reflections \\(U_{Y},U_{Z}\\) lie on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).  \nThe external median through \\(X\\) meets \\((XYZ)\\) again at the midpoint of the arc \\(YZ\\) not containing \\(X\\); the external symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of that external median, hence it is the polar of \\(X\\) with respect to the circle \\((U_{Y}U_{Z}X)\\).  \nA polar is tangent to its circle at the pole, therefore the external symmedian is tangent to \\((U_{Y}U_{Z}X)\\). ∎  \n\nApplying Lemma 2 to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain:\n\n> The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the external symmedian through \\(P\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the reflections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) together with the vertex \\(P\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Identification of the reflected points with \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(M_{1}\\) be the reflection of \\(M\\) in the side \\(PN\\);  \n* \\(N_{1}\\) be the reflection of \\(N\\) in the side \\(PM\\).\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to these circles gives  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}= AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{MC}{ND}. \\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(MC=MA\\) and \\(ND=NA\\); therefore (6.2) rewrites as  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{MA}{NA}. \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\nA standard computation shows that the reflections \\(M_{1},N_{1}\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nAM_{1}=AE,\\qquad AN_{1}=AF . \\tag{6.4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the quadrilateral \\(BEFM_{1}N_{1}\\) is cyclic and its circumcircle coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThus the circle described in Lemma 2 is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Final step\n\nFrom Lemma 1 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\).  \nLemma 2 tells us that this external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the reflections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\).  \nBy the identification made in §6 this circumcircle is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved. ∎"
