not** pass through \\(A\\) in the general configuration (the problem statement does not assert collinearity of \\(A,M,N\\)). Therefore the claim that the homothety “fixes the whole line \\(MN\\)” is false.  \n\nBecause the line is not invariant, we cannot assert that the image of the point \\(C\\in MN\\) is the point \\(D\\in MN\\). In fact, the image of \\(C\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) lies on the line \\(AC\\), not on \\(MN\\).  \n\n**Conclusion:** Critical Error – the homothety does not map \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and does not fix line \\(MN\\). This invalidates every subsequent statement that relies on (1.1).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Mapping of the circle \\((ACD)\\)\n\n> “Since \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes \\(A\\), it maps the circle \\((ACD)\\) onto itself; therefore the centre \\(P\\) lies on the invariant line \\(AP\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThis statement depends entirely on the false claim that \\(\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D\\). Because that mapping is incorrect, we cannot deduce that the circle through \\(A,C,D\\) is invariant. Moreover, even if a homothety fixes two distinct points of a circle, it would fix the whole circle **only if the homothety is the identity**, which is not the case here (the ratio is not 1). Hence the reasoning is invalid.\n\n**Conclusion:** Critical Error – the invariance of \\((ACD)\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) is not established.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Claim that \\(M,N\\) lie on \\((ACD)\\)\n\n> “Because \\(M,N\\) belong to the circle \\((ACD)\\), we have \\(PM=PN\\); hence the segment \\(PO\\) (where \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\)) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThere is no justification that the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) of the original circles lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). In the general configuration they are interior points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, and need not be concyclic with \\(A,C,D\\). The statement is simply false unless the configuration is