onjugate of that external median, hence it is the polar of \\(X\\) with respect to the circle \\((U_{Y}U_{Z}X)\\). A polar is tangent to its circle at the pole …”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe proof invokes advanced concepts (polar, isogonal conjugate) without any justification that the required conditions hold. In particular:\n\n* It is not shown that the external median meets the circumcircle at the midpoint of the opposite arc, a fact that itself requires a proof.\n* The statement “the external symmedian is the polar of \\(X\\) with respect to the circle \\((U_{Y}U_{Z}X)\\)” is a non‑trivial claim that needs a lemma; none is supplied.\n* Even if the polar statement were true, the conclusion that the polar is tangent at the pole is a property of *the polar of a point with respect to a circle*, not of a *symmedian*.\n\nThus the argument is incomplete; the result may be true, but the solution does not provide a rigorous derivation.\n\n**Conclusion:** Justification Gap – the lemma is asserted without a complete proof.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Identification of reflected points with \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n> “Because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(A\\) … gives \\(AE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}= AF\\cdot AD\\).  \n> … Using (2.3) we obtain \\(\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{MC}{ND}\\).  \n> … A standard computation shows that the reflections \\(M_{1},N_{1}\\) satisfy \\(AM_{1}=AE,\\; AN_{1}=AF\\). Consequently the quadrilateral \\(BEFM_{1}N_{1}\\) is cyclic …”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The power‑of‑a‑point relation \\(AE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}\\) is correct **only** if \\(B\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(A\\) and the centre of the circle, which is not the case here; \\(B\\) is the other intersection of the two circles, not a point on line \\(AE\\) or \\(AF\\). Hence the equality is unjustified.\n* The step “\\(\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{MC}{ND}\\)” uses the earlier (incorrect) ratio (2.3) that relied on the false homothety mapping \\(C\\) to \\(D\\). Consequently the derived proportion is baseless.\n* The claim that the reflections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) satisfy \\(AM_{1}=AE\\) etc. is presented as a “standard computation” but no computation is shown. Without a proof, the equality remains an unverified assertion.\n* The cyclicity of \\(BEFM_{1}N_{1}\\) is deduced from the previous (unproved) equalities; no angle or power argument is supplied.\n\n**Conclusion:** Justification Gap – several key equalities and the cyclicity claim are asserted without proof; they rely on earlier false statements, so the identification of the reflected points with \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) is not established.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Final step\n\n> “From Lemma 1 … Lemma 2 … By the identification made in §6 … the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nSince Lemma 1 is false, Lemma 2 is not proved, and the identification in §6 is unverified, the final conclusion rests on a chain of invalid arguments. Therefore the statement is not proved.\n\n**Conclusion:** The final result is not established; the proof collapses.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution contains **multiple critical errors** (incorrect use of homothety, false claim that \\(M,N\\) lie on \\((ACD)\\), erroneous symmedian argument) that break the logical flow. In addition, several steps are presented with **justification gaps** (Lemma 2, the identification of reflected points). Because the core geometric framework is flawed, the proof does not establish the required tangency. Hence the solution is **invalid**."
