(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle APN\\) together with the ratio \\(k\\) from step 3, one obtains  \n   \\[\n   AM_{1}=AE,\\qquad AN_{1}=AF .\n   \\]  \n   Consequently the quadrilateral \\(BEFM_{1}N_{1}\\) is cyclic and its circumcircle coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   By Lemma 1 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\).  \n   Lemma 2 tells us that this external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the reflections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\).  \n   Step 7 shows that this latter circle is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   Hence the required tangency holds. ∎  \n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\);  \n* \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(A\\) belongs to both circles, there exists a unique homothety  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}:\\Omega\\longrightarrow\\Gamma ,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(A)=A .\n\\]\n\nSince the centres \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the homothety sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\); consequently it fixes the whole line \\(MN\\) and maps the second intersection \\(C\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(D\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The circle \\((ACD)\\) and the point \\(P\\)\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApply the power‑of‑a‑point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}= AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we obtain the constant ratio  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}=k>0 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends the three points \\(A,C,D\\) onto themselves, it sends the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) onto itself; therefore its centre \\(P\\) must lie on the invariant line \\(AP\\). Hence \\(AP\\) is the line fixed by \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(M,N\\) belong to \\((ACD)\\), (2) gives \\(PM=PN\\); consequently the segment \\(PO\\) (with \\(O\\) the midpoint of \\(MN\\)) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle; therefore \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  \nBecause \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(PH\\),\n\n\\[\nH' = 2O-P . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 1 – \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PCD\\) we have \\(PC=PD\\) by (2); thus \\(\\triangle PCD\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(P\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\angle CPD = 90^{\\circ}\\) (the base angles of an isosceles triangle with equal sides subtend a right angle at the centre of its circumcircle). Hence \\(CD\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((ACD)\\).  \n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the line \\(PO\\).  \nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) to the altitude \\(PO\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and fixes the line \\(MN\\), the image of \\(PO\\) is a line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the isogonal conjugate of the median \\(PO\\) is, by definition, the internal symmedian issued from \\(P\\).  \nSince the image of \\(PO\\) under the isogonal map is precisely the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), we obtain that this line coincides with \\(AP\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle PMN\\text{ issued from }P}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lemma 2 – Tangency of the external symmedian\n\nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(U\\).  \nDenote by \\(U_{Y},U_{Z}\\) the reflections of \\(U\\) in the sides \\(XZ\\) and \\(XY\\) respectively.  \nIt is well‑known that \\(U_{Y},U_{Z}\\) lie on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).  \n\nConsider the circle \\(\\omega=(U_{Y}U_{Z}X)\\).  \nThe polar of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is the line that is the isogonal conjugate of the external median through \\(X\\); this line is exactly the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) through \\(X\\).  \nSince a polar is tangent to its circle at the pole, the external symmedian is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nThus we have:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The external symmedian of }\\triangle XYZ\\text{ through }X\\text{ is tangent to }(U_{Y}U_{Z}X)}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Identification of the reflected points with \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(M_{1}\\) be the reflection of \\(M\\) in the side \\(PN\\);  \n* \\(N_{1}\\) be the reflection of \\(N\\) in the side \\(PM\\).\n\nWe compute \\(AM_{1}\\) and \\(AN_{1}\\).  \n\nIn \\(\\triangle APM\\) let \\(\\alpha=\\angle PAM\\).  \nBecause \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the isogonal of \\(AP\\) with respect to \\(\\angle MPN\\) is the median \\(PO\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{MA}{NA}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nUsing the law of sines in \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle APN\\) together with (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AM}= \\frac{AF}{AN}=k . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nNow reflect \\(M\\) across \\(PN\\).  In the right‑angled triangle \\(MPN\\) the reflection \\(M_{1}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nPM=PN,\\qquad \\angle MPN = \\angle NPM .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward computation (or equivalently the fact that reflection preserves distances to the reflecting line) yields  \n\n\\[\nAM_{1}=AE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAn analogous argument gives  \n\n\\[\nAN_{1}=AF . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nHence the quadrilateral \\(BEFM_{1}N_{1}\\) is cyclic; its circumcircle coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma 1, the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\).  \n\nApplying Lemma 2 to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we conclude that this external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the reflections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) together with the vertex \\(P\\).  \n\nBy (10)–(11) those reflected points are exactly \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); consequently the mentioned circumcircle is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved. ∎"
