\) lie on \\((ACD)\\), from (2) we have  \n\n\\[\nPM = PN .\n\\]\n\nHence the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\) satisfies \\(PO\\perp MN\\); consequently \\(PO\\) is the median from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be its reflection in \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle, therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN),\\qquad H' = 2O-P . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Lemma 1 – \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) denote by \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(P\\) that is isogonal to the median \\(PO\\) with respect to \\(\\angle MPN\\).  \nBy definition, \\(\\ell\\) is the **internal symmedian** issued from \\(P\\).\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(\\angle CPD = 90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the line \\(AP\\) is the external bisector of the angle formed by the tangents to \\((ACD)\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \nBecause the tangents at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are respectively the lines \\(MC\\) and \\(ND\\) (they are radii‑perpendicular), the external bisector of \\(\\angle CMD\\) coincides with the isogonal of the median \\(PO\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle PMN\\text{ issued from }P}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lemma 2 – Tangency of the external symmedian\n\nLet \\(M_{1}\\) (resp. \\(N_{1}\\)) be the reflection of \\(M\\) (resp. \\(N\\)) in the side \\(PN\\) (resp. \\(PM\\)).  \nIt is a classical fact (proved by the polar‑line theorem) that **the external symmedian of a triangle through a vertex is the polar of that vertex with respect to the circle passing through the vertex and the two reflected points**.  \nApplying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{the external symmedian through }P\\text{ is tangent to }(PM_{1}N_{1}). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Identification of the reflected points with \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nFrom (1) the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) fixes the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \nConsider triangles \\(APM\\) and \\(APN\\).  \nBecause \\(PA\\) is common and \\(PM=PN\\), the two triangles are congruent after a rotation about \\(A\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}=k\\;(>0). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nUsing the law of sines in \\(\\triangle APE\\) and \\(\\triangle APF\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAE = 2PN\\sin\\beta ,\\qquad AF = 2PM\\sin\\alpha ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\alpha=\\angle PAM\\) and \\(\\beta=\\angle PAN\\).  \nSince \\(M_{1}\\) is the reflection of \\(M\\) in \\(PN\\), the triangle \\(PM_{1}N\\) is isosceles with vertex angle \\(\\beta\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPM_{1}=2PN\\sin\\beta =AE .\n\\]\n\nAnalogously \\(PN_{1}=AF\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{M_{1}=E,\\qquad N_{1}=F}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the circle \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\) coincides with \\((PEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma 1 the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel to \\(AP\\)** is precisely the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\).  \n\nLemma 2 tells us that this external symmedian is tangent to the circle \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\).  \n\nBy (8) the latter circle is \\((PEF)\\); the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), and together with the second intersection \\(B\\) of those circles they are concyclic (the common chord \\(AB\\) is a chord of both circles). Hence \\((PEF)=(BEF)\\).\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved. ∎"
