e with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\);  \n* \\(M_{1}\\) (resp. \\(N_{1}\\)) be the reflection of \\(M\\) (resp. \\(N\\)) in the side \\(PN\\) (resp. \\(PM\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Inversion at \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AB^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(AB\\) is a common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the inversion interchanges the two circles:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\Gamma,\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\Omega .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal I(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal I(E)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal I(F)=F .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The circle \\((ACD)\\) and the point \\(P\\)\n\nThe circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) passes through the three points \\(A,C,D\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal I\\) fixes \\(A\\) and swaps \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), the image of \\((ACD)\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is again \\((ACD)\\).  \nTherefore the centre \\(P\\) of this circle is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nThe only points invariant under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) are the points of the line \\(AP\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nP\\in AP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends the chord \\(CD\\) onto itself, the chord is orthogonal to the line joining the