ine  \n\n\\[\nM_{1}= \\text{reflection of }M\\text{ across }PN,\\qquad \nN_{1}= \\text{reflection of }N\\text{ across }PM .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1 applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to the internal symmedian \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is tangent at \\(P\\) to the circle \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent at }P\\text{ to }(PM_{1}N_{1}).} \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Identification \\(M_{1}=E\\) and \\(N_{1}=F\\)\n\nBecause \\(M_{1}\\) is the reflection of \\(M\\) in the line \\(PN\\),\n\n\\[\nPM_{1}=PM,\\qquad \\angle MPM_{1}=2\\angle NPN =2\\angle APN .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly \\(PN_{1}=PN\\) and \\(\\angle NPN_{1}=2\\angle APM\\).\n\nConsider triangles \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle APM_{1}\\). They share the side \\(AP\\); by (10) the angle at \\(P\\) in the latter is twice the angle at \\(P\\) in the former, i.e. \\(\\angle M_{1}PA = \\angle APN\\). Using the law of sines in \\(\\triangle APM_{1}\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM_{1}}{AE}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AEM_{1}}{\\sin\\angle AM_{1}E}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle AEM_{1}= \\angle AEM = \\angle APM\\) (since \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(M\\) is its centre) and \\(\\angle AM_{1}E = \\angle AME = \\angle APN\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM_{1}}{AE}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle APM}{\\sin\\angle APN}= \\frac{AM}{AN} \\quad\\text{(by (7))}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}\\); therefore \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{PM_{1}}{AE}= \\frac{AE}{AF}\\). Since \\(PM_{1}=PM=PN\\) and \\(AE\\neq 0\\), we deduce \\(PM_{1}=AE\\). Moreover the direction of \\(PM_{1}\\) coincides with that of \\(PA\\) (both make the same angle with \\(PN\\)), so \\(M_{1}\\) lies on the ray \\(AP\\) at distance \\(AE\\) from \\(A\\); that point is precisely \\(E\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nM_{1}=E. \\tag{11}\n\