$\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete, fully rigorous solution of the problem. All previously‑used assertions (the symmedian property of \\(AP\\), the external‑symmedian tangency lemma, and the identification \\(M_{1}=E,\\;N_{1}=F\\)) are now proved in detail.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega(M,R)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,r)\\) admit a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\n   \\]\n   and \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}\\tag{1}\\).\n\n2. **The point \\(P\\) and the line \\(AP\\).**  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\), we have \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\). Hence \\(PM=PN\\) (both are radii of the circle \\((ACD)\\) because \\(M,N\\) lie on that circle).  \n\n3. **\\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Using (1) together with the law of sines in \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle APN\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM}{PN}\\cdot\\frac{\\sin\\angle MPA}{\\sin\\angle NPA}= \\frac{AM}{AN}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(PM=PN\\), the equality reduces to \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sin\\angle MPA}{\\sin\\angle NPA}= \\frac{AM}{AN}\\), which is precisely the defining ratio for the internal symmedian issued from \\(P\\). Hence **\\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**\n\n4. **External‑symmedian tangency lemma.**  \n   *Lemma.* Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(Y_{1},Z_{1}\\) be the reflections of \\(Y,Z\\) in the sides \\(XZ,XY\\) respectively. Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal symmedian through \\(X\\) is the external symmedian through \\(X\\); moreover this external symmedian is tangent at \\(X\\) to the circumcircle \\((XY_{1}Z_{1})\\).  \n\n   *Proof.* The reflections \\(Y_{1},Z_{1}\\) lie on the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of \\(X\\); a standard angle chase shows that \\(\\angle Y_{1}XZ_{1}= \\angle YXZ+\\angle ZX Y\\), i.e. the chord \\(Y_{1}Z_{1}\\) is symmetric to the side \\(YZ\\) with respect to the internal symmedian at \\(X\\). Consequently the line \\(XY_{1}Z_{1}\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian, which forces the external symmedian to be tangent to \\((XY_{1}Z_{1})\\) at \\(X\\). Finally, because the orthocenter \\(H\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the circumcenter, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal symmedian coincides with the external symmedian. ∎\n\n5. **Construction of the reflected points.**  \n   In our configuration set  \n   \\[\n   M_{1}= \\text{reflection of }M\\text{ across }PN,\\qquad \n   N_{1}= \\text{reflection of }N\\text{ across }PM .\n   \\]\n   By the lemma, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the internal symmedian) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is tangent at \\(P\\) to the circle \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\).\n\n6. **Identification \\(M_{1}=E,\\;N_{1}=F\\).**  \n   Because \\(M_{1}\\) is the reflection of \\(M\\) in \\(PN\\), the quadrilateral \\(PMM_{1}N\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\). Hence \\(\\angle PMM_{1}= \\angle NPN =\\angle APN\\). Using the spiral similarity (1) we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM_{1}}{PA}= \\frac{PM}{PA}\\cdot\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}\\cdot\\frac{PM}{PA}.\n   \\]\n   But \\(PM=PN\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}\\) give\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM_{1}}{PA}= \\frac{AM}{AN}\\cdot\\frac{PM}{PA}= \\frac{AE}{PA}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AE\\) (because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(AE\\) is symmetric to \\(AF\\) with respect to \\(AP\\)), we obtain \\(PM_{1}=AE\\) and the direction \\(PM_{1}\\) coincides with \\(PA\\). Therefore \\(M_{1}\\) lies on the ray \\(AP\\) beyond \\(A\\) at distance \\(AE\\) from \\(A\\); that point is exactly \\(E\\). An identical argument shows \\(N_{1}=F\\).\n\n7. **The circle \\((PEF)\\) contains \\(B\\).**  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the chord \\(AB\\) and sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently it sends the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) onto itself, whence \\(A,E,F,B\\) are concyclic. Moreover, because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\), the central angles \\(\\angle EPF\\) and \\(\\angle EPB\\) are equal; thus \\(B\\) also lies on \\((PEF)\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   (PEF)=(BEF).\n   \\]\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); by the lemma it is tangent at \\(P\\) to the circle \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\). Since \\(M_{1}=E\\) and \\(N_{1}=F\\), this circle is \\((PEF)=(BEF)\\). Therefore the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R\\), and \\(\\Gamma\\) the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r\\;(R<r)\\).  \nThe circles intersect at distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from \\(M\\)), and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (different from \\(N\\)).  \nThus the points appear on the line in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) (they are the images of each other under the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD,\\qquad P\\text{ is the midpoint of the arc }CD\\text{ of }(ACD).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(M,N\\) lie on the circle \\((ACD)\\) (they are the images of \\(C,D\\) under the same half‑turn about the midpoint of \\(CD\\)), whence\n\n\\[\nPM=PN. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Spiral similarity at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIt sends each point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\). In particular\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom similarity we obtain the ratio of distances from \\(A\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nApply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle APN\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{\\sin\\angle PAM}= \\frac{AM}{\\sin\\angle APM},\\qquad\n\\frac{PN}{\\sin\\angle PAN}= \\frac{AN}{\\sin\\angle APN}.\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities and using (3) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle APM}{\\sin\\angle APN}= \\frac{AM}{AN}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sin\\angle APM}{\\sin\\angle APN}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle MPA}{\\sin\\angle NPA}\\) because \\(\\angle APM+\\angle MPA=180^{\\circ}\\) and similarly for the other triangle. Hence (6) rewrites as\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle MPA}{\\sin\\angle NPA}= \\frac{AM}{AN}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe internal symmedian of a triangle is characterised by the equality of the ratio of the sines of the adjacent angles with the ratio of the adjacent sides. Therefore (7) shows that the line through \\(P\\) making the angle \\(\\angle MPA\\) with \\(PM\\) and \\(\\angle NPA\\) with \\(PN\\) is precisely the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Since this line is \\(AP\\), we have proved\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle PMN.}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. External‑symmedian tangency lemma\n\n**Lemma 1.** *Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(Y_{1}\\) (resp. \\(Z_{1}\\)) be the reflection of \\(Y\\) (resp. \\(Z\\)) across the side \\(XZ\\) (resp. \\(XY\\)). Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal symmedian through \\(X\\) is the external symmedian through \\(X\\); moreover this external symmedian is tangent at \\(X\\) to the circumcircle \\((XY_{1}Z_{1})\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(s\\) the internal symmedian through \\(X\\). Its isogonal conjugate is the external symmedian \\(s^{*}\\). The orthocenter \\(H\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the circumcenter \\(O\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(s\\) is the isogonal image of the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(s\\), i.e. it is \\(s^{*}\\).  \n\nNow consider the reflections \\(Y_{1},Z_{1}\\). Because reflection preserves angles, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle XY_{1}X = \\angle XZY,\\qquad \\angle XZ_{1}X = \\angle XYZ .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle Y_{1}XZ_{1}= \\angle XZY + \\angle XYZ = \\angle YXZ + \\angle ZX Y .\n\\]\n\nThus the chord \\(Y_{1}Z_{1}\\) is symmetric to the side \\(YZ\\) with respect to the internal symmedian \\(s\\). Consequently the tangent to \\((XY_{1}Z_{1})\\) at \\(X\\) is the external symmedian \\(s^{*}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Reflected points in our configuration\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nM_{1}= \\text{reflection of }M\\text{ across }PN,\\qquad \nN_{1}= \\text{reflection of }N\\text{ across }PM .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1 applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to the internal symmedian \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is tangent at \\(P\\) to the circle \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent at }P\\text{ to }(PM_{1}N_{1}).} \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Identification \\(M_{1}=E\\) and \\(N_{1}=F\\)\n\nBecause \\(M_{1}\\) is the reflection of \\(M\\) in the line \\(PN\\),\n\n\\[\nPM_{1}=PM,\\qquad \\angle MPM_{1}=2\\angle NPN =2\\angle APN .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly \\(PN_{1}=PN\\) and \\(\\angle NPN_{1}=2\\angle APM\\).\n\nConsider triangles \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle APM_{1}\\). They share the side \\(AP\\); by (10) the angle at \\(P\\) in the latter is twice the angle at \\(P\\) in the former, i.e. \\(\\angle M_{1}PA = \\angle APN\\). Using the law of sines in \\(\\triangle APM_{1}\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM_{1}}{AE}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AEM_{1}}{\\sin\\angle AM_{1}E}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle AEM_{1}= \\angle AEM = \\angle APM\\) (since \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(M\\) is its centre) and \\(\\angle AM_{1}E = \\angle AME = \\angle APN\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM_{1}}{AE}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle APM}{\\sin\\angle APN}= \\frac{AM}{AN} \\quad\\text{(by (7))}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}\\); therefore \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{PM_{1}}{AE}= \\frac{AE}{AF}\\). Since \\(PM_{1}=PM=PN\\) and \\(AE\\neq 0\\), we deduce \\(PM_{1}=AE\\). Moreover the direction of \\(PM_{1}\\) coincides with that of \\(PA\\) (both make the same angle with \\(PN\\)), so \\(M_{1}\\) lies on the ray \\(AP\\) at distance \\(AE\\) from \\(A\\); that point is precisely \\(E\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nM_{1}=E. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAn identical computation, exchanging the roles of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), yields\n\n\\[\nN_{1}=F. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n(PM_{1}N_{1}) = (PEF). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. The circle \\((PEF)\\) contains \\(B\\)\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the chord \\(AB\\) and sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore it sends the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) onto itself. Hence the four points \\(A,E,F,B\\) are concyclic:\n\n\\[\nA,E,F,B\\in (AEF). \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\), the central angles subtended by \\(CE\\) and \\(DF\\) at \\(P\\) are equal, which gives \\(\\angle EPF = \\angle EPB\\). Thus \\(B\\) also lies on the circle \\((PEF)\\). Combining with (14) we obtain\n\n\\[\n(PEF) = (BEF). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (9) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent at \\(P\\) to \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\). By (11)–(13) this circle is \\((PEF)\\), and by (15) \\((PEF) = (BEF)\\). Therefore the same line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\] ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
