-----------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  A lemma on the external symmedian\n\n> **Lemma 5.1.**  \n> Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\).  Let \\(Y_{1}\\) (resp. \\(Z_{1}\\))\n> be the reflection of \\(Y\\) (resp. \\(Z\\)) in the side \\(XZ\\) (resp. \\(XY\\)).\n> Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal symmedian through\n> \\(X\\) coincides with the external symmedian through \\(X\\); moreover this\n> external symmedian is tangent at \\(X\\) to the circumcircle \\((XY_{1}Z_{1})\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the\nexternal symmedian.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the\ncircumcentre \\(O\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the\ninternal symmedian is the isogonal image of the line through \\(O\\) parallel\nto the internal symmedian, i.e. it is the external symmedian.  \n\nReflecting \\(Y\\) in \\(XZ\\) and \\(Z\\) in \\(XY\\) preserves the angles with the\nsides, whence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle XY_{1}X = \\angle XZY,\\qquad\n\\angle XZ_{1}X = \\angle XYZ .\n\\]\n\nAdding gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle Y_{1}XZ_{1}= \\angle XZY+\\angle XYZ\n                 = \\angle YXZ+\\angle ZXY .\n\\]\n\nThus the chord \\(Y_{1}Z_{1}\\) is symmetric to the side \\(YZ\\) with respect to\nthe internal symmedian; consequently the tangent to \\((XY_{1}Z_{1})\\) at \\(X\\)\nis the external symmedian. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Application of the lemma to \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM_{1}= \\text{reflection of }M\\text{ in }PN,\\qquad\nN_{1}= \\text{reflection of }N\\text{ in }PM .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 5.1 applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain:\n\n* the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to the\n  internal symmedian \\(AP\\) is the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* this external symmedian is tangent at \\(P\\) to the circumcircle \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\).\n\nHence the required line is tangent at \\(P\\) to \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\). \\tag{6.1}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Identification of the reflected points\n\nBecause \\(M_{1}\\) is the reflection of \\(M\\) in \\(PN\\), the quadrilateral\n\\(PMM_{1}N\\) is an isosceles trapezoid; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PMM_{1}= \\angle NPN = \\angle APN . \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PNN_{1}= \\angle MPM = \\angle APM . \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nConsider triangles \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle APM_{1}\\).  \nBoth share the side \\(AP\\); by (7.1) they have equal angles at \\(P\\).\nTherefore they are similar, and from (2.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM_{1}}{PA}= \\frac{AE}{AF}. \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\nBut (2.2) gives \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}\\), and using\n(3.7) we have \\(PM_{1}=PM=PA\\cdot\\frac{AE}{AF}=AE\\).  Moreover the direction\nof \\(PM_{1}\\) coincides with that of \\(PA\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nM_{1}=E . \\tag{7.4}\n\\]\n\nAn entirely analogous argument yields  \n\n\\[\nN_{1}=F . \\tag{7.5}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n(PM_{1}N_{1}) = (PEF). \\tag{7.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  The circle \\((PEF)\\) also passes through \\(B\\)\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the chord \\(AB\\) and sends\n\\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore it sends the circumcircle of\n\\(\\triangle AEF\\) onto itself.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nA,E,F,B\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\),\nthe central angles \\(\\angle EPF\\) and \\(\\angle EPB\\) are equal; consequently\n\\(B\\) also lies on \\((PEF)\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n(PEF) = (BEF). \\tag{8.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (6.1) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent at \\