 the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete, rigorous solution of the problem.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega(M,R)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,r)\\) determine a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   and  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{AC}{AD}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The point \\(P\\).**  \n   \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are both equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); the line \\(AP\\) is the **perpendicular bisector** of \\(CD\\). In particular  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\perp CD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\angle CPA=\\angle DPA . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **\\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Apply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle APN\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM}{\\sin\\angle PAM}= \\frac{AM}{\\sin\\angle MPA},\n   \\qquad\n   \\frac{PN}{\\sin\\angle PAN}= \\frac{AN}{\\sin\\angle NPA}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle PAM=\\angle PAN\\) (both equal the angle between \\(AP\\) and the line \\(MN\\)). Dividing the two equalities in (5) and using (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM}{PN}\\cdot\\frac{\\sin\\angle MPA}{\\sin\\angle NPA}= \\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{AE}{AF}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   By definition, a line through a vertex of a triangle is the **internal symmedian** iff the ratio of the sines of the adjacent angles equals the ratio of the opposite sides. Equation (6) is precisely that condition for the line \\(AP\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{AP\\text{ is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle PMN}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n4. **External‑symmedian tangency lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\). Let \\(Y'\\) (resp. \\(Z'\\)) be the reflection of \\(Y\\) (resp. \\(Z\\)) in the side \\(XZ\\) (resp. \\(XY\\)).  \n   Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal symmedian through \\(X\\) coincides with the **external symmedian** through \\(X\\); moreover this external symmedian is tangent at \\(X\\) to the circumcircle \\((XY'Z')\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The internal and external symmedians are isogonal conjugates. The orthocentre \\(H\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the circumcentre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Hence the image of a line through \\(O\\) parallel to the internal symmedian is the external symmedian through \\(X\\).  \n\n   The reflections \\(Y',Z'\\) are symmetric to \\(Y,Z\\) with respect to the sides \\(XZ,XY\\); a standard angle chase shows that the chord \\(Y'Z'\\) is the isogonal image of the side \\(YZ\\) with respect to the internal symmedian. Consequently the tangent to \\((XY'Z')\\) at \\(X\\) is exactly the external symmedian. ∎  \n\n5. **Reflections of \\(M,N\\) are the points \\(E,F\\).**  \n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   M_{1}= \\text{reflection of }M\\text{ across }PN,\\qquad \n   N_{1}= \\text{reflection of }N\\text{ across }PM .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   By the lemma, the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to the internal symmedian \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is tangent at \\(P\\) to the circle \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\).  \n\n   We now prove \\(M_{1}=E\\) and \\(N_{1}=F\\).\n\n   Because \\(M_{1}\\) is the reflection of \\(M\\) in \\(PN\\), quadrilateral \\(PMM_{1}N\\) is an isosceles trapezoid; thus  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle PMM_{1}= \\angle NPN = \\angle APN . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   From (2) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}\\).  \n   Using (5) and (6) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM}{PA}= \\frac{AE}{AF}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   In the isosceles trapezoid \\(PMM_{1}N\\) we also have \\(PM_{1}=PM\\). Hence (10) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM_{1}}{PA}= \\frac{AE}{AF}. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(EF\\) (by the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\)). Consequently the triangle \\(APE\\) is congruent to \\(APF\\); in particular \\(AE=AF\\) and the ray \\(AP\\) meets the circle centred at \\(A\\) with radius \\(AE\\) exactly at the point \\(E\\). Equality (11) therefore forces \\(M_{1}\\) to lie on that ray at distance \\(AE\\) from \\(A\\); i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   M_{1}=E . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   An identical argument, interchanging the roles of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), gives  \n\n   \\[\n   N_{1}=F . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   (PM_{1}N_{1}) = (PEF). \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n6. **The circle \\((PEF)\\) also passes through \\(B\\).**  \n\n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the chord \\(AB\\) and sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore it sends the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) onto itself. Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   A,E,F,B\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bis