ius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe final answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega(M,R)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,r)\\) determine a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   and the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\).\n\n2. **\\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Using the power‑of‑a‑point relation \\(AM\\cdot AN = AC\\cdot AD\\) (which follows from the definition of \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\)) and the law of sines in \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle APN\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM}{PN}\\cdot\\frac{\\sin\\angle MPA}{\\sin\\angle NPA}= \\frac{AM}{AN},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the ratio of the sines of the angles adjacent to \\(AP\\) equals the ratio of the opposite sides; this is precisely the characterisation of an internal symmedian. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\text{ is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle PMN .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A lemma on orthocenter, symmedian and reflected points.**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\). Let \\(Y'\\) (resp. \\(Z'\\)) be the reflection of \\(Y\\) (resp. \\(Z\\)) in the side \\(XZ\\) (resp. \\(XY\\)). Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal symmedian through \\(X\\) coincides with the external symmedian through \\(X\\) and is tangent at \\(X\\) to the circumcircle \\((XY'Z')\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Denote by \\(O\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). The internal symmedian through \\(X\\) is the isogonal image of the median \\(XM\\) (\\(M\\) the midpoint of \\(YZ\\)). Because \\(H\\) is the isogonal conjugate of \\(O\\), the isogonal image of a line through \\(O\\) parallel to the internal symmedian is the external symmedian through \\(X\\).  \n\n   The reflections \\(Y',Z'\\) are obtained from \\(Y,Z\\) by reflecting in the sides through \\(X\\); therefore the chord \\(Y'Z'\\) is the isogonal image of the side \\(YZ\\) with respect to the internal symmedian at \\(X\\). Consequently the tangent to the circumcircle \\((XY'Z')\\) at \\(X\\) is exactly the external symmedian. ∎  \n\n4. **Identification of the reflected points with \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   M_{1}= \\text{reflection of }M\\text{ across }PN,\\qquad \n   N_{1}= \\text{reflection of }N\\text{ across }PM .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   By the Lemma, the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to the internal symmedian \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is tangent at \\(P\\) to the circle \\((PM_{1}N_{1})\\).\n\n   Because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(D\\), it sends the line \\(PN\\) (the image of \\(PM\\) after the reflection in the internal symmedian) to the line \\(PE\\). Hence the image under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) of the reflection of \\(M\\) in \\(PN\\) is the