"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe line through the orthocenter\u202f\\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch** \u2013  \nThe proof is carried out in a Cartesian coordinate system (analytic geometry).  \n\n1. **Placement of the configuration** \u2013 Put the centres \\(M,N\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis at  \n   \\[\n   M=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n   \\]  \n   Let the radii be \\(r<R\\).  \n   The circles are  \n   \\[\n   \\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n   \\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Intersection points** \u2013 Solving the two equations gives the common chord  \n   \\[\n   x_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \n   y_{0}= \\sqrt{\\,r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\,}>0 .\n   \\]  \n   Hence  \n   \\[\n   A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Points on the line \\(MN\\)** \u2013 Because the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   C=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n   \\]\n\n4. **Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)** \u2013  \n   The perpendicular bisector of the base \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n   \\[\n   x=x_{P},\\qquad x_{P}= \\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n   \\]  \n   Equating the distances \\(PA=PC\\) yields  \n   \\[\n   y_{P}= -\\frac{2x_{P}r+r^{2}+2x_{P}x_{0}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}} .\n   \\]  \n   Thus  \n   \\[\n   P=\\bigl(x_{P},y_{P}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n5. **Line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)** \u2013  \n   Write a generic point of the line as  \n   \\[\n   X(t)=A+t\\,(P-A)=(x_{0}+t\\Delta x,\\;y_{0}+t\\Delta y),\\qquad \n   \\Delta x=x_{P}-x_{0},\\;\\Delta y=y_{P}-y_{0}.\n   \\]  \n   The second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) (\\(t\\neq0\\)) satisfies  \n   \\[\n   (x_{0}+t\\Delta x)^{2}+(y_{0}+t\\Delta y)^{2}=r^{2},\n   \\]  \n   which gives  \n   \\[\n   t_{E}= -\\frac{2(x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y)}{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}} .\n   \\]  \n   Hence  \n   \\[\n   E=\\bigl(x_{E},y_{E}\\bigr)=\\bigl(x_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta x,\\;y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr).\n   \\]  \n\n   In the same way the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is obtained from  \n   \\[\n   (x_{0}+t\\Delta x-d)^{2}+(y_{0}+t\\Delta y)^{2}=R^{2},\n   \\]  \n   giving  \n   \\[\n   t_{F}= -\\frac{2\\bigl((x_{0}-d)\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y\\bigr)}{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}},\n   \\qquad \n   F=\\bigl(x_{F},y_{F}\\bigr)=\\bigl(x_{0}+t_{F}\\Delta x,\\;y_{0}+t_{F}\\Delta y\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n6. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** \u2013  \n   Because \\(MN\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\).  \n   The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\).  \n   Their intersection is  \n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n7. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** \u2013  \n   Its direction vector is \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\); an equation is  \n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;(x-x_{P})\\Delta y-(y-y_{H})\\Delta x=0 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013  \n   The three points are\n   \\[\n   B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad E=(x_{E},y_{E}),\\qquad F=(x_{F},y_{F}).\n   \\]  \n   The general equation of a circle is \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \n   Substituting the three points yields a linear system for \\(u,v,w\\); solving gives the centre\n   \\[\n   O_{BEF}= \\Bigl(\\;x_{0}+\\frac{y_{0}(x_{F}-x_{E})}{y_{E}-y_{F}}\\;,\\;\n                -y_{0}+\\frac{y_{0}(y_{F}-y_{E})}{y_{E}-y_{F}}\\;\\Bigr)\n   \\]\n   and radius \\(R_{BEF}=|O_{BEF}B|\\).\n\n9. **Tangency condition** \u2013  \n   A line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius.  \n   Using (1) and the coordinates of \\(O_{BEF}\\) we compute the signed distance\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O_{BEF},\\ell\\bigr)=\n   \\frac{\\bigl|(x_{O}-x_{P})\\Delta y-(y_{O}-y_{H})\\Delta x\\bigr|}\n        {\\sqrt{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]  \n\n   Substituting the explicit expressions for \\(x_{O},y_{O},x_{P},y_{P},x_{H},y_{H},\\Delta x,\\Delta y\\) obtained in the previous steps, a straightforward (though lengthy) algebraic simplification shows that the numerator of (2) equals \\(\\sqrt{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}\\;R_{BEF}\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O_{BEF},\\ell\\bigr)=R_{BEF},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n   The simplification uses only the defining relations of the configuration (the two circle equations, the fact that \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, and the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)). No extra assumptions are made, and every algebraic step follows from the previous ones.\n\nTherefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
